- Non‑surgical treatments (scaling and root planing) are effective for early‑to‑moderate gum disease and are generally covered by dental insurance. Surgical options (flap surgery, bone grafting) may be needed for advanced cases, but costs vary widely; always request a written treatment plan and check benefits before proceeding.
- Medicare and many Medicaid plans do not cover routine periodontal care unless deemed medically necessary. Private dental insurance may have annual maximums and waiting periods. For uninsured patients, federally qualified health centers (FQHCs), dental schools, and sliding‑fee clinics offer more affordable options.
