Dear Asian Health Hub community,

October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month—a time to prioritize early detection and preventive care. For Asian American families, navigating healthcare can feel overwhelming, especially when language barriers stand in the way of understanding screening options. This issue of *AHH Pulse* is dedicated to helping you take charge of your breast health with practical, culturally sensitive advice and tools to find language-accessible mammogram services. We also celebrate the growth of our directory with new pediatric clinics that support Vietnamese, Korean, and Chinese speakers. Together, we can make preventive care more accessible for every generation.

## 5 Crucial Health Tips for Breast Cancer Screening for Asian Women

**1. Know Your Risk Factors—They May Differ for Asian Women**
Asian women often have a lower body mass index and higher breast density, which can make tumors harder to detect on mammograms. Additionally, family history of breast or ovarian cancer, certain genetic mutations (like BRCA), and lifestyle factors such as a diet high in processed soy products (though moderate whole soy is safe) may influence risk. Talk to your doctor about your personal and family history to create a tailored screening plan.

**2. Start Screening at Age 40—Or Earlier if You Have Risk Factors**
The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force recommends biennial mammograms starting at age 40 for average-risk women. However, if you have a strong family history or are of Ashkenazi Jewish descent (common among some Asian subgroups), you may need to begin earlier. Do not wait for symptoms—early detection saves lives.

**3. Choose a Facility That Offers Language Support**
Many imaging centers now provide interpretation services or have multilingual staff. Ask if they offer materials in Chinese, Korean, Vietnamese, or other languages. This ensures you fully understand the preparation, procedure, and follow-up instructions. Our directory includes clinics that prioritize language access—look for the “Languages” tag when scheduling.

**4. Bring a Family Member or Friend to Help Translate**
If you are more comfortable with your native language, bring a trusted loved one who can help communicate with the medical team. Some facilities allow you to request a professional interpreter in advance—do not hesitate to ask. Clear communication reduces anxiety and improves the accuracy of your care.

**5. Understand Breast Density and Why It Matters**
Asian women are more likely to have dense breast tissue, which appears white on a mammogram—similar to tumors. This means mammograms alone may miss some cancers. If you have dense breasts, ask your provider about supplemental screening such as ultrasound or MRI. Knowing your breast density is your right; in many states, you will receive a notice after your mammogram.

## Spotlight: Newly Verified Clinics Added This Month

Our directory continues to grow with trusted providers who speak your language. This month, we are proud to highlight pediatric clinics—because breast health awareness also means caring for the whole family’s well-being. These clinics are now available for you to book with confidence.

| Clinic Name | Specialty | Location | Language Support |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Children''s Health Center (4D) | Pediatrics | None | English, Chinese |
| Providence Pediatrics - Redondo Beach | Pediatrics | Redondo Beach, CA | English, Korean |
| Fountain Valley Pediatrics | Pediatrics | None | English, Vietnamese |
| PM Pediatric Urgent Care | Pediatrics | Tustin, CA | English, Vietnamese |
| Pediatric & Adult Medicine | Pediatrics | Tustin, CA | English, Vietnamese |

## Tips for Navigating Healthcare without Language Barriers

**1. Check Insurance and Language Support Before Your Visit**
Call your insurance provider or the clinic directly to confirm whether interpretation services are covered. Many plans offer free telephonic or in-person interpreters for scheduled appointments. Ask specifically, “Do you have staff who speak Vietnamese/Korean/Chinese?”

**2. Prepare Bilingual Care Notes**
Write down your symptoms, questions, and medical history in both English and your native language. Use reliable health glossaries or translation apps (like MedlinePlus in multiple languages) to ensure accuracy. Bring these notes to your appointment so nothing gets lost in translation.

**3. Verify Interpreter Availability Before Booking**
When scheduling a mammogram or any procedure, ask for a certified medical interpreter if you need one. Some clinics require advance notice. If a clinic lists “English, Vietnamese” in our directory, that means they have staff who speak the language—but it is always best to confirm at the time of booking.

## Medical Disclaimer

