## Tips for Navigating Healthcare without Language Barriers

**1. Check insurance and language services before booking.**
When calling a clinic, ask directly: “Do you have staff who speak [your language]?” or “Is a medical interpreter available for my appointment?” Many clinics offer free interpreter services by phone or video, but it’s best to confirm ahead of time. Also, verify that the clinic accepts your insurance plan to avoid surprise bills.

**2. Prepare a bilingual care note for your visit.**
Write down your child’s symptoms, medications, and questions in both English and your home language. This helps you communicate clearly with the provider and ensures nothing is lost in translation. You can use a simple template: “My child has had [symptom] for [duration]. We have tried [treatment]. Our main concern is [question].”

**3. Request a written summary after the visit.**
Ask the doctor or nurse to provide discharge instructions, medication names, and follow-up steps in writing—preferably in English and your preferred language. Many clinics now offer translated handouts or can print instructions in multiple languages. Keep this summary for your records and share it with other family caregivers.

