Dear Asian Health Hub community,

As we welcome the first days of spring, March is also Colorectal Cancer Awareness Month — a time to talk openly about a topic that many of us in Vietnamese, Korean, and Chinese American families prefer to avoid. In our communities, discussing bowel health or scheduling a colonoscopy can feel uncomfortable, even taboo. But the truth is, colorectal cancer is one of the most preventable cancers when caught early. And with language-accessible care, you don’t have to face this alone.

In this issue of AHH Pulse, we’ll walk you through what a colonoscopy really involves, share tips to overcome cultural barriers, and introduce five new verified clinics that speak your language. Let’s take this step together — for your health and your family’s peace of mind.

## 5 Crucial Health Tips for Colorectal Cancer Screening

**1. Start screening at age 45 — or earlier if you have a family history.**
Many Asian Americans assume they are too young for colorectal cancer, but rates are rising in people under 50. If you have a parent, sibling, or child who had colorectal cancer or polyps, talk to your doctor about starting screening even earlier. Early detection saves lives.

**2. A colonoscopy is not as scary as you think — and it’s the gold standard.**
The preparation (the “cleanout”) is the hardest part, but the procedure itself is done under sedation, so you won’t feel pain. Most people sleep through it and wake up with no memory of the exam. The entire appointment usually takes less than two hours, and you can return to normal activities the next day.

**3. Don’t let embarrassment or cultural stigma stop you from talking to your doctor.**
In many Asian cultures, discussing bowel movements or rectal exams is considered private or shameful. But your doctor has seen and heard it all — they are there to help, not judge. Bring a trusted family member or request a same-language provider to make the conversation easier.

**4. If you can’t get a colonoscopy, ask about at-home stool tests.**
For those who are not ready for a colonoscopy, non-invasive options like the fecal immunochemical test (FIT) or Cologuard can be done at home. These tests detect blood or abnormal DNA in stool. However, if the result is positive, you will still need a colonoscopy. Talk to your provider about which option is right for you.

**5. Prepare a bilingual health history and medication list before your appointment.**
Bring a written list of your medications, allergies, and any family history of cancer — in both English and your native language. This helps ensure nothing is lost in translation. Many clinics now offer interpretation services, but having your own notes can make the visit smoother and more accurate.

