## Tips for Navigating Healthcare without Language Barriers

**1. Check Insurance & Language Services Before Booking**
When calling a clinic, ask directly: “Do you have a staff interpreter who speaks [Korean/Vietnamese/Chinese]?” Many clinics now offer tele-interpretation devices. Also confirm whether the clinic accepts your insurance plan—especially important for those on Medicare or state-funded plans.

**2. Prepare a Bilingual Care Notes Card**
Write down key symptoms, medications, and questions in both English and your native language. Keep a copy in your wallet or phone. This helps even if you see a provider who doesn’t speak your language. You can use free translation apps for assistance.

**3. Bring a Multilingual Family Member or Request a Medical Interpreter**
Federal law requires hospitals and clinics receiving federal funds to provide language assistance. You have the right to a qualified interpreter (not just a family member) for important conversations about diagnoses or procedures. Don’t hesitate to request one during scheduling.

