Dear Asian Health Hub community,

Happy New Year! As we step into 2026, it''s a time for renewal and planning. This month, we focus on care planning for the whole family—from our beloved seniors navigating Medicare and Medicaid updates to the little ones needing pediatric care. Language-accessible healthcare remains a cornerstone of our mission, and we''re excited to share new resources to help you start the year healthy and informed.

## 5 Crucial Health Tips for New Year Care Planning

**1. Review Your Health Insurance Plans for 2026**
Medicare open enrollment ended December 7, but you may still qualify for a Special Enrollment Period if you moved, lost coverage, or experienced other life changes. For those on Medicaid, redeterminations continue—check your state''s renewal deadlines and update your contact information to avoid gaps. For families with employer-sponsored plans, review your Summary of Benefits to understand copays, deductibles, and network changes.

**2. Schedule Annual Wellness Visits and Preventive Screenings**
Prevention is key. For seniors, this includes blood pressure checks, cholesterol panels, diabetes screenings, and cancer screenings (mammograms, colonoscopies). For children, well-child visits ensure vaccinations are up to date and developmental milestones are met. Many insurance plans cover these at no cost—use them!

**3. Create a Bilingual Family Health Record**
Prepare a simple document listing each family member''s medications, allergies, chronic conditions, and emergency contacts—in both English and your home language (Vietnamese, Korean, or Chinese). This helps during doctor visits, especially if an interpreter is needed. Keep a copy on your phone and in your wallet.

**4. Plan for Multi-Generational Caregiving**
Many Asian households care for elders and children under one roof. Discuss advance directives (living wills, healthcare proxies) with seniors to ensure their wishes are respected. Coordinate care schedules and share responsibilities among family members. Consider using a shared calendar or app to track appointments.

**5. Stay Active and Connected with Culturally Appropriate Activities**
Physical activity reduces fall risk and improves mental health. Encourage seniors to join community tai chi, walking groups, or gentle yoga classes. For children, outdoor play and family walks are great. Social connection is vital—attend cultural events or virtual gatherings to combat isolation.

