## Tips for Navigating Healthcare without Language Barriers

1. **Check Insurance and Language Support Before Booking**
   When calling a clinic, ask: "Do you have staff who speak [your language]?" Also confirm your insurance plan is accepted. Many Asian‑focused clinics now offer telephone interpretation services — request this in advance.

2. **Prepare a Bilingual Care Note**
   Write down your symptoms, medications, and questions in both English and your heritage language. Bring it to every appointment. This helps reduce miscommunication and ensures your concerns are fully addressed.

3. **Verify Interpreter Services at the Appointment**
   Even if a clinic lists a language, confirm upon arrival that a professional medical interpreter is available — not a family member (who may feel uncomfortable or miss medical terms). You have the right to a qualified interpreter at no cost.

## Medical Disclaimer

The information in this newsletter is for educational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always consult a qualified healthcare provider with any questions regarding your health or a medical condition. If you think you are having a medical emergency, call 911 immediately.

Stay heart‑healthy this February — and every month.
Warmly,  
